WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.720
Module 3: planning and running fieldwork.

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:08.028
The seven teams go first, and what they
find shapes what everyone else is asked.

00:00:08.028 --> 00:00:12.009
So these three weeks decide the
quality of everything after.

00:00:12.009 --> 00:00:17.051
In about forty minutes, you'll plan a
team's fieldwork, judge what they log,

00:00:17.051 --> 00:00:21.629
and keep seven teams moving to a
synthesis you can build a report on.

00:00:22.133 --> 00:00:26.231
Every team workspace opens with
its domain guide: a focus,

00:00:26.231 --> 00:00:29.199
four research questions and three methods.

00:00:29.199 --> 00:00:31.460
The guide says what to find out.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:34.427
A fieldwork plan says how, in three weeks.

00:00:34.427 --> 00:00:36.829
A good plan answers six questions.

00:00:36.829 --> 00:00:38.384
Which questions first?

00:00:38.384 --> 00:00:39.797
Who will we talk to?

00:00:39.797 --> 00:00:42.976
What documents and
artifacts will we collect?

00:00:42.976 --> 00:00:45.167
What will we watch happen live?

00:00:45.167 --> 00:00:47.004
When will we do each part?

00:00:47.004 --> 00:00:48.841
And how will we record it?

00:00:48.841 --> 00:00:53.151
A simple rhythm works: interviews
and collection in week one,

00:00:53.151 --> 00:00:57.037
more interviews and a first
look at themes in week two,

00:00:57.037 --> 00:01:00.287
then the gaps and the
synthesis in week three.

00:01:00.287 --> 00:01:02.336
Write it down before kickoff.

00:01:02.833 --> 00:01:06.497
Each domain guide already
points at the right method.

00:01:06.497 --> 00:01:11.681
The HR Systems guide says to interview
managers and individual contributors

00:01:11.681 --> 00:01:16.105
separately about the same processes,
and compare their accounts.

00:01:16.105 --> 00:01:20.045
So Northwind's team mapped
scheduling and the float pool,

00:01:20.045 --> 00:01:23.570
then heard from managers
and frontline staff apart.

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:29.308
The History guide says to interview different
tenure cohorts about the same events,

00:01:29.308 --> 00:01:34.492
so that team talked to long-serving
staff, people who joined with Lakeside,

00:01:34.492 --> 00:01:35.667
and recent hires.

00:01:35.667 --> 00:01:39.607
Artifacts walks the space with
an insider and a newcomer.

00:01:39.607 --> 00:01:41.542
Rituals attends events live.

00:01:41.542 --> 00:01:46.588
And in a health system, Stakeholder
Relations' boundary roles are patient

00:01:46.588 --> 00:01:51.081
experience, referrals, the ambulance
liaison and the front desks.

00:01:51.567 --> 00:01:56.522
Fieldwork isn't a survey, so you're
not after a representative sample.

00:01:56.522 --> 00:02:02.326
You're after range: enough different vantage
points that no single voice carries a

00:02:02.326 --> 00:02:02.750
theme.

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:06.573
Build it on four axes: site,
level, tenure, and shift.

00:02:06.573 --> 00:02:09.970
Talk to frontline staff
and managers separately.

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:13.014
Nights and weekends
tell different stories.

00:02:13.014 --> 00:02:17.119
And avoid the easy sample: the
people who volunteer first,

00:02:17.119 --> 00:02:22.145
the people researchers already know,
and the people nearest the office.

00:02:22.145 --> 00:02:26.675
Ask managers to release people's
time, not to choose who speaks.

00:02:27.167 --> 00:02:29.931
Before every interview, say four things.

00:02:29.931 --> 00:02:32.281
What the dig is, and who it's for.

00:02:32.281 --> 00:02:36.498
That taking part is voluntary,
and they can stop at any time.

00:02:36.498 --> 00:02:41.060
That their words may be quoted by
role and context, never by name,

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:44.170
and nobody they mention
will be named either.

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:47.142
And who reads the notes:
the research team,

00:02:47.142 --> 00:02:50.183
the program leads and
the program's advisor.

00:02:50.183 --> 00:02:52.741
Then ask for a story, not an opinion.

00:02:53.233 --> 00:02:58.513
Here are six real-looking entries from
Northwind's HR Systems workspace.

00:02:58.513 --> 00:03:03.206
Mark each one good, fix, or move
to somewhere else, and say why.

00:03:03.206 --> 00:03:05.772
Then compare with the model answer.

00:03:06.267 --> 00:03:10.359
The product puts it plainly:
the description does the work.

00:03:10.359 --> 00:03:13.204
A photo with no explanation is a picture.

00:03:13.204 --> 00:03:17.574
A photo plus "every award on this
wall is for individual sales,

00:03:17.574 --> 00:03:19.794
none for teamwork" is a finding.

00:03:19.794 --> 00:03:24.164
Northwind's Artifacts team found
two intake forms still in use,

00:03:24.164 --> 00:03:26.522
one from each legacy organization.

00:03:26.522 --> 00:03:30.199
People joke about the blue
forms and the green forms.

00:03:30.199 --> 00:03:32.558
That's an artifact with a meaning.

00:03:32.558 --> 00:03:35.540
Get permission before
photographing people.

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:39.841
In a clinic, that means never a
patient, a chart, or a screen.

00:03:39.841 --> 00:03:45.252
And remember that anyone who can read
the program's findings can open what you

00:03:45.252 --> 00:03:45.738
upload.

00:03:46.233 --> 00:03:49.368
The synthesis is the
team's real contribution.

00:03:49.368 --> 00:03:54.547
Whatever is in the box when they submit
becomes that domain's section of the

00:03:54.547 --> 00:03:58.158
report, and it's the text
the analysis leans on most.

00:03:58.158 --> 00:04:01.156
Lead with what you found,
then the evidence.

00:04:01.156 --> 00:04:04.700
Three well-evidenced patterns
beat ten observations.

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:10.083
And say where the evidence is thin:
Northwind's HR team wrote that they'd heard

00:04:10.083 --> 00:04:14.512
four accounts about promotion and
couldn't corroborate a pattern.

00:04:14.512 --> 00:04:17.851
That honesty is worth more
than false confidence.

00:04:17.851 --> 00:04:22.894
Submit only when it's ready, because
submitting locks the whole workspace,

00:04:22.894 --> 00:04:25.483
and only a Program Lead can reopen it.

00:04:25.483 --> 00:04:30.798
A team that hasn't submitted is treated
as provisional when the analysis runs.

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:33.843
Here's how to keep seven teams moving.

00:04:33.843 --> 00:04:35.382
First, plan your dates.

00:04:35.382 --> 00:04:40.133
Pick a start, press Suggest dates,
and you get findings at three weeks,

00:04:40.133 --> 00:04:44.483
stories closing at five, analysis
at six and the report at eight.

00:04:44.483 --> 00:04:48.431
Nothing is locked by the plan,
but it drives the reminders.

00:04:48.431 --> 00:04:52.647
Then press Start fieldwork, with
Email every researcher ticked,

00:04:52.647 --> 00:04:57.599
and each team gets an email with its
deadline and a link to its workspace.

00:04:57.599 --> 00:05:01.145
Watch the running picture
at the top of the Overview.

00:05:01.145 --> 00:05:06.499
Each team is reminded three days before
its deadline and again once it's passed,

00:05:06.499 --> 00:05:09.243
and you're told when a team goes overdue.

00:05:09.243 --> 00:05:13.592
If you need to nudge by hand, each
team card has a Remind button.

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:15.044
May the third.

00:05:15.044 --> 00:05:16.797
Five teams have submitted.

00:05:16.797 --> 00:05:19.493
The Stories team asks for two more days,

00:05:19.493 --> 00:05:22.931
because they're unsure
about one of their patterns.

00:05:22.931 --> 00:05:24.549
What do you advise Alex?

00:05:25.033 --> 00:05:28.223
If a submitted team needs
to revise something,

00:05:28.223 --> 00:05:30.996
a Program Lead can reopen the workspace.

00:05:30.996 --> 00:05:33.977
Ask them to submit
again when they're done.

00:05:33.977 --> 00:05:36.681
And once a program has passed wave one,

00:05:36.681 --> 00:05:40.564
check the amber notice in the
workspace before you type.

00:05:40.564 --> 00:05:45.486
Writing this wave's fieldwork into
the last wave's workspace is silent,

00:05:45.486 --> 00:05:47.081
and it can't be undone.

00:05:47.567 --> 00:05:52.243
Time for the module check:
six questions, five to pass.

00:05:52.733 --> 00:05:58.920
Here's the Domain Fieldwork Plan, one for
each team: questions, people, artifacts,

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:01.712
events, schedule and recording rules.

00:06:01.712 --> 00:06:04.880
You'll use it again in the practical task.

00:06:05.367 --> 00:06:06.156
To sum up.

00:06:06.156 --> 00:06:09.548
Turn each domain guide
into a written plan.

00:06:09.548 --> 00:06:14.912
Sample for range, and never have
people interview their own reports.

00:06:14.912 --> 00:06:19.724
Observations never hold a name,
documents go in as artifacts,

00:06:19.724 --> 00:06:22.485
and the description is the finding.

00:06:22.485 --> 00:06:27.455
A synthesis leads with what was
found and says where it's thin.

00:06:27.455 --> 00:06:31.399
And let the plan and the
reminders do the chasing.

00:06:31.399 --> 00:06:33.213
Next: story collection.
